<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Auersperg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Auersperg family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Auersperg is an Austrian noble family originating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, at the castle of Auersperg (Turjak) in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. Its members served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> as soldiers and statesmen, and in 1653 the line of Johann Weikhard von Auersperg was raised to princely rank. In 1654 the family received the Silesian Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Münsterberg</a>. Later members included the Austrian prime ministers Karl and Adolf von Auersperg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-auersperg.jpg" alt="House of Auersperg" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auersperg-Fuersten-Wappen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first certain member of the family is Engelbertus de Ursberch, a witness to a deed of 1162 issued by Herman, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>. Its seat was Auersperg Castle south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, built in 1067 according to an engraving on site, beneath the original arms showing an aurochs, Auerochs in German and Tur in Slovene. The family may have come from Ursberg in Swabia and probably settled in Lower Carniola after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Battle of Lechfeld</a> in 955; its estates stretched from Grosuplje to Velike Lašče and Ribnica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1407 the Auerspergs held the office of hereditary chamberlain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, and in the 15th century the family split into the lines of Auersperg Castle and Schönberg. Its members fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>: Trojan of Auersperg at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529, Andreas von Auersperg, called the Carniolan Achilles, at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a> in 1593, and Herbard VIII von Auersperg, commanding general of the Croatian and Slavonian Military Frontier, who fell in battle in 1575 and whose severed head was taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><p>Johann Weikhard of Auersperg took part in the negotiations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> and was tutor to Ferdinand IV, after whose election as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> he was raised to imperial prince. In 1664 he acquired Tengen, which gave the house an unconditional seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-princes">Council of Princes</a>, and he served as prime minister to Emperor Leopold I from 1665 to 1669. In 1791 the family sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Münsterberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and its county of Gottschee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> was raised to a duchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The annexation of Tengen by the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1806 ended the princes' rule as territorial lords, but as a mediatised house they kept equality of birth with the reigning houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the order of precedence of 1837 placed them fifth among the mediatised houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1861 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> made Karl, 8th Prince of Auersperg, the first president of the Austrian House of Lords. The poet Anastasius Grün, Count Anton Alexander von Auersperg, also belonged to the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Auersperg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1654 the emperor granted it to the <b>Auersperg family</b>, who seldom lived there.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ziebice">Duchy of Ziębice</a></li></ul></section>
